IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `acat-dates`

-- ACT Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2008 -- the questions of the person bringing (or answering) an application
-- to the ACT Civil and Administrative Tribunal (ACAT).
--
-- SEAT: the individual who brings a claim to ACAT, or answers one: the person with a civil dispute (a debt, a contract,
-- goods or services, a damages claim for negligence such as a small personal injury claim, a nuisance or trespass) worth
-- up to $25 000, or the person who wants a government decision reviewed under an authorising law. "Can ACAT hear my
-- claim?", "By when must I apply?", "Must I be given reasons, and by when?", "Who pays the costs?", "What happens if I do
-- not turn up?", "Can I appeal?", "How do I enforce the order?". The tribunal, the president, the registrar, the
-- decision-maker and the Minister get no seat: what they must do is encoded as what the person must be given and by
-- when, and what they may decide is asked as a fact ("if the tribunal is satisfied that ...").
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. The two duties with deadlines that the person meets (the applicant's duty to apply for review within 28 days,
-- s 10; the decision-maker's duty to give a reasons statement within 28 days of a request, s 22B) are regulative rules
-- run by EVALTRACE inside the questions.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: Part 3 (ss 9-12, applications, the review time limit, deemed refusals); Part 4 (ss 15-22, civil
-- disputes and the $25 000 limit); from Part 4A, ss 22B, 22D, 22T (reasons statements, the additional statement, legal
-- and financial assistance); from Part 5, ss 47A, 48, 48A, 49 (absence, costs), s 53 (interim orders), s 54 (2)
-- (representations on deciding without a hearing), ss 59, 60 (copy of order, statement of reasons); ss 67A, 67B (notice
-- of reviewable decisions); Part 7 ss 69A, 71, 74 (enforcement, failure to comply); Part 8 ss 79, 80 (1), 82A, 83, 86
-- (appeals, removal). Out, and why: NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: ACT Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2008, A2008-35, Republication No 42 (R42), effective 23 February
-- 2026, deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/A2008-35.txt. No regulation or tribunal rule is deposited: where the
-- Act leaves a number or a period to them, it is asked as a fact.
--
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to the tribunal, the president or the
-- Minister are INPUTS ("if the tribunal considers ..."). Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and
-- 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): an answer that is the text's own no is marked "(the text's no)"; an answer where the text is
-- limited by its own wording, or silent, is marked "NOTHING-kind".

§ `ACT Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2008: the questions of the person bringing or answering an application`

-- The parties to the two regulative rules, and the steps.
DECLARE `Party` IS ONE OF
    `the applicant`
    `the decision-maker`

DECLARE `Step` IS ONE OF
    `lodge the application for review`
    `give the reasons statement`

-- s 10 (2): "An application to the tribunal for review of a decision must be made by a person within 28 days after the
-- day the decision to be reviewed is made." The days run from the day in s 10 (2) or (3) that applies (see below).
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Step`
`s 10 -- apply for review within the time allowed` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the applicant`
    MUST `lodge the application for review`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the applicant` BECAUSE "the application for review was not made within 28 days (or the longer period the rules prescribe) after the day s 10 counts from"

-- s 22B (2): "The decision-maker must give the applicant a written reasons statement for the decision within 28 days after
-- the day the applicant asks for the statement unless ..."
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Step`
`s 22B -- give the reasons statement within 28 days` MEANS
    PARTY `the decision-maker`
    MUST `give the reasons statement`
    WITHIN 28
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the decision-maker` BECAUSE "no reasons statement within 28 days after the day the applicant asked for it (s 22B (2))"

-- Runs a rule over one event on a day (counted from day 0). FULFILLED means in time. Not exported; takes a DEONTIC only
-- internally.
GIVEN `the rule` IS A DEONTIC `Party` `Step`, `the party` IS A `Party`, `the step` IS A `Step`, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `in time under` `the rule` `the party` `the step` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `the rule` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the party` `the step` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Timing` IS ONE OF
    `done in time`
    `not yet done, still in time`
    `out of time`

-- Whether a step taken (or, if not yet taken, still to be taken) is in time. A step taken before the period starts is
-- counted as taken on day 0.
GIVEN `the rule` IS A DEONTIC `Party` `Step`, `the party` IS A `Party`, `the step` IS A `Step`,
      `done` IS A BOOLEAN, `done on` IS A DATE, `today` IS A DATE, `the start` IS A DATE, `the deadline` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Timing`
`the timing under` `the rule` `the party` `the step` `done` `done on` `today` `the start` `the deadline` MEANS
    IF `done`
    THEN IF `in time under` `the rule` `the party` `the step` `the day done`
         THEN `done in time`
         ELSE `out of time`
    ELSE IF `the deadline` `falls before` `today`
         THEN `out of time`
         ELSE `not yet done, still in time`
    WHERE
        `the raw day` MEANS `the days from` `the start` `to` `done on`
        `the day done` MEANS IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw day`

§§ `Part 3 -- Applications to the tribunal (ss 9-12)`

-- s 9: "A person may apply to the tribunal if an authorising law provides that the application may be made."
-- s 10 (1): "An application to the tribunal must -- (a) comply with the rules; and (b) be in writing, whether with or
-- without a registrar's help; and (c) state the reasons for making the application; and (d) be lodged at the tribunal
-- registry." Whether an authorising law (another ACT law, or this Act for a civil dispute) allows the application, and
-- whether it complies with the tribunal's rules (not deposited), are asked. Fork F1: the four requirements read as
-- conditions of a properly made application; one missing is the text's no.
@export Is my application to the ACT Civil and Administrative Tribunal properly made (ACAT Act 2008 ss 9, 10 (1))? You may apply if an authorising law (for a civil dispute, this Act) allows the application, and the application must comply with the tribunal's rules, be in writing, state your reasons and be lodged at the tribunal registry.
GIVEN `an authorising law allows it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a territory law provides that you may make this application to ACAT (for a civil dispute such as a debt, contract, goods, damages, nuisance or trespass claim, this Act itself does)
      `complies with the rules` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the application complies with the tribunal's rules (the ACAT procedure rules: the approved form, the fee and so on)
      `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the application is in writing (a registrar may help you write it)
      `states my reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the application states your reasons for making it
      `lodged at the registry` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the application has been lodged at the tribunal registry
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 10 (1) -- is my application properly made` `an authorising law allows it` `complies with the rules` `in writing` `states my reasons` `lodged at the registry` IF
        `an authorising law allows it`
    AND `complies with the rules`
    AND `in writing`
    AND `states my reasons`
    AND `lodged at the registry`

-- s 12 (1), (2): if the decision-maker, required or allowed to do something under an authorising law, has not done it
-- within the period for doing it (or, if no period is stated, a reasonable period), and the authorising law lets a
-- person apply for review of a decision about doing it, the decision-maker "is taken to have decided, at the end of the
-- period for doing the thing, not to do the thing". The "if" conditions failing are the text's no. What a reasonable
-- period is is a judgment: its last day is asked as a fact.
@export Is the decision-maker taken to have refused, so that I can apply to ACAT for review (ACAT Act 2008 s 12)? If a decision-maker who is required or allowed to do something under an authorising law has not done it within the period for doing it (or a reasonable period, if none is stated), and that law lets you apply for review, the decision-maker is taken to have decided not to do it at the end of the period.
GIVEN `required or allowed to act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision-maker is required or allowed to do the thing you asked for under an authorising law (another ACT law that gives ACAT review)
      `review of that is available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that law provides that a person may apply to ACAT for review of a decision about doing the thing
      `done within the period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision-maker did the thing within the period for doing it
      `the period ends on` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the period the authorising law states for doing the thing, or, if it states none, the last day of a reasonable period for doing it
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 12 -- is the decision-maker taken to have refused` `required or allowed to act` `review of that is available` `done within the period` `the period ends on` `today` IF
        `required or allowed to act`
    AND `review of that is available`
    AND (NOT (`done within the period`))
    AND `the period ends on` `falls before` `today`

-- How the person learned of the decision, for s 10 (2) and (3).
DECLARE `Notice of the decision` IS ONE OF
    `I was given notice of the decision`
    `notice of the decision had to be given to me but was not`
    `no notice had to be given to me, and none was`
    `the decision is taken to have been made under s 12 (nothing was done within the period)`

-- s 10 (2), (3) and s 25 (1) (e), (2), (3): the last day to apply for review.
--   (2) within 28 days after the day the decision is made;
--   (3) (a) if notice is given later than 5 days after the day the decision is made, within 28 days after the day the
--       notice is given; (b) if notice is required but not given, within 28 days after the day the person becomes aware of
--       the decision; (c) for a decision taken to be made under s 12, within 28 days after the end of the period.
--   s 25 (2): a rule that prescribes a longer time displaces the 28 days; s 25 (3): not where the authorising law says
--   the thing cannot be done in the longer time.
-- Fork F3: s 10 (3) opens with "However": the later of the s 10 (2) day and the s 10 (3) day is taken. Fork F4: a longer
-- period under the rules is counted from the same day.
GIVEN `the decision was made on` IS A DATE, `notice` IS A `Notice of the decision`, `notice given on` IS A DATE,
      `aware on` IS A DATE, `days the rules allow` IS A NUMBER, `longer period barred` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DATE
`the day s 10 counts from` `the decision was made on` `notice` `notice given on` `aware on` `days the rules allow` `longer period barred` MEANS
    CONSIDER `notice`
    WHEN `I was given notice of the decision` THEN
        IF `the days from` `the decision was made on` `to` `notice given on` GREATER THAN 5
        THEN `notice given on`
        ELSE `the decision was made on`
    WHEN `notice of the decision had to be given to me but was not` THEN `the later of` `the decision was made on` `and` `aware on`
    WHEN `no notice had to be given to me, and none was` THEN `the decision was made on`
    WHEN `the decision is taken to have been made under s 12 (nothing was done within the period)` THEN `the decision was made on`

GIVEN `days the rules allow` IS A NUMBER, `longer period barred` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the days to apply for review` `days the rules allow` `longer period barred` MEANS
    IF `days the rules allow` GREATER THAN 28 AND (NOT (`longer period barred`))
    THEN `days the rules allow`
    ELSE 28

@export By when must I apply to ACAT for review of a decision (ACAT Act 2008 s 10 (2), (3), s 25 (2), (3))? Within 28 days after the day the decision is made; or, if notice reached you more than 5 days after the decision, within 28 days after the notice; or, if notice was required but never given, within 28 days after you became aware of it; or, for a decision taken to be made under s 12, within 28 days after the period ended. A longer period in the tribunal's rules applies unless the authorising law forbids it.
GIVEN `the decision was made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision was made (for a decision taken to be made under s 12 because nothing was done, the last day of the period for doing it)
      `notice` IS A `Notice of the decision` @desc Whether you were given notice of the decision, whether notice had to be given to you, or whether the decision is one taken to have been made under s 12
      `notice given on` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given notice of the decision (if you were not, any date; it is not used)
      `aware on` IS A DATE @desc The day you became aware of the decision (used only if notice had to be given to you but was not)
      `days the rules allow` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the tribunal's rules allow for applying for review of this kind of decision, if they prescribe a period longer than 28 days; otherwise 0
      `longer period barred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the authorising law under which you apply says the application cannot be made in a longer period (for example, Planning Act 2023 s 507 (4) for third-party reviews)
GIVETH A DATE
`s 10 -- the last day to apply for review` `the decision was made on` `notice` `notice given on` `aware on` `days the rules allow` `longer period barred` MEANS
    `the start` `plus days` `the days`
    WHERE
        `the start` MEANS `the day s 10 counts from` `the decision was made on` `notice` `notice given on` `aware on` `days the rules allow` `longer period barred`
        `the days` MEANS `the days to apply for review` `days the rules allow` `longer period barred`

DECLARE `Am I in time to apply for review` IS ONE OF
    `in time: you applied on or before the last day s 10 allows`
    `still in time: you have not applied, and the last day s 10 allows has not passed`
    `out of time under s 10 (the text's no): the application for review was not made within the period`

@export Am I (or was I) in time to apply to ACAT for review of a decision (ACAT Act 2008 s 10 (2), (3))? An application for review must be made within 28 days after the day the decision is made, or the later day s 10 (3) counts from, or within a longer period the tribunal's rules prescribe.
GIVEN `the decision was made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision was made (for a decision taken to be made under s 12 because nothing was done, the last day of the period for doing it)
      `notice` IS A `Notice of the decision` @desc Whether you were given notice of the decision, whether notice had to be given to you, or whether the decision is one taken to have been made under s 12
      `notice given on` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given notice of the decision (if you were not, any date; it is not used)
      `aware on` IS A DATE @desc The day you became aware of the decision (used only if notice had to be given to you but was not)
      `days the rules allow` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the tribunal's rules allow for applying for review of this kind of decision, if they prescribe a period longer than 28 days; otherwise 0
      `longer period barred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the authorising law under which you apply says the application cannot be made in a longer period
      `applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already lodged the application for review
      `applied on` IS A DATE @desc The day you lodged it (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not yet applied)
GIVETH A `Am I in time to apply for review`
`s 10 -- am I in time to apply for review` `the decision was made on` `notice` `notice given on` `aware on` `days the rules allow` `longer period barred` `applied` `applied on` `today` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the timing under` (`s 10 -- apply for review within the time allowed` `the days`) `the applicant` `lodge the application for review` `applied` `applied on` `today` `the start` (`the start` `plus days` `the days`)
    WHEN `done in time` THEN `in time: you applied on or before the last day s 10 allows`
    WHEN `not yet done, still in time` THEN `still in time: you have not applied, and the last day s 10 allows has not passed`
    WHEN `out of time` THEN `out of time under s 10 (the text's no): the application for review was not made within the period`
    WHERE
        `the start` MEANS `the day s 10 counts from` `the decision was made on` `notice` `notice given on` `aware on` `days the rules allow` `longer period barred`
        `the days` MEANS `the days to apply for review` `days the rules allow` `longer period barred`

§§ `Part 4 -- Civil disputes (ss 15-22)`

-- s 15 and s 16 (1): "civil dispute application means an application that consists of 1 or more of the following
-- applications: (a) a contract application; (b) a damages application; (c) a debt application; (d) a goods application;
-- (e) a nuisance application; (f) a trespass application; (g) an application for a debt declaration; (h) an application
-- for a common boundaries determination; (i) an application for an order under the Australian Consumer Law (ACT); (j) an
-- application stated to be a civil dispute application in an authorising law." s 16 (2): "a civil dispute application
-- does not include a fair work claim." A "means" definition and a flat negative: the text's no outside the list.
-- Plain gloss of s 15: "damages application" is a claim for damages for negligence or any other tort except nuisance or
-- trespass (so a personal injury claim in negligence is one); "goods application" covers goods or services supplied,
-- and damages for detaining goods or for their return.
DECLARE `What my claim is` IS ONE OF
    `a claim about a contract, including damages for breach of contract`
    `a claim for damages for negligence or another tort, other than nuisance or trespass to land`
    `a claim to recover a debt`
    `a claim about goods or services supplied, including damages for detaining goods or for their return`
    `a claim for relief for nuisance`
    `a claim for relief for trespass to land`
    `a claim for a declaration that I am, or am not, indebted to the other party (or for a stated amount)`
    `a claim for a determination under the Common Boundaries Act 1981 (or a variation of one)`
    `a claim for an order under the Australian Consumer Law (ACT)`
    `a claim that another territory law states to be a civil dispute application`
    `a fair work claim (under the Magistrates Court Act 1930 s 266E)`
    `something else`

@export Is my claim a "civil dispute application" that I can bring to ACAT (ACAT Act 2008 ss 15, 16, 17)? A civil dispute application is a contract, damages (negligence or other tort, other than nuisance or trespass), debt, goods or services, nuisance or trespass to land claim, a debt declaration, a common boundaries determination, an order under the Australian Consumer Law (ACT), or one another territory law calls a civil dispute application; never a fair work claim.
GIVEN `my claim` IS A `What my claim is` @desc What your claim is for (if it is several, pick any one of them that fits the list)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 16 -- is my claim a civil dispute application` `my claim` IF
        (NOT (`my claim` EQUALS `a fair work claim (under the Magistrates Court Act 1930 s 266E)`))
    AND (NOT (`my claim` EQUALS `something else`))

-- s 18 (2): "The tribunal's jurisdiction is limited to -- (a) civil dispute applications claiming amounts of not more than
-- $25 000; or (b) in relation to debt declarations -- applications for declarations for debts of not more than
-- $25 000."
`the jurisdictional limit (s 18 (2))` MEANS 25000

DECLARE `Can ACAT hear my claim for this amount` IS ONE OF
    `not a civil dispute application, so Part 4 and its limit do not apply (the text's no)`
    `yes: the $25 000 limit does not apply to this application (s 18 (3))`
    `yes: the amount counted is not more than $25 000 (s 18 (2))`
    `yes, the whole amount: the parties agree to ACAT deciding it and the tribunal is satisfied they understand the excess over $25 000 is not abandoned (s 21)`
    `yes, but only for $25 000: you abandon the excess by limiting your claim to $25 000, and the excess cannot be recovered (s 20)`
    `no: the amount counted is more than $25 000 and neither s 20 nor s 21 allows it (the text's no, s 18 (1))`

-- s 18 (1): "A civil dispute application cannot be made to the tribunal for an amount greater than the tribunal's
-- jurisdictional limit, unless section 20 ... or section 21 ... allows the application to be made."
-- s 18 (3): the limit does not apply to (a) a common boundaries determination, (b) an application prescribed by
-- regulation (no regulation is deposited: asked).
-- s 19 (1): interest, and a lump sum instead of interest, are disregarded. s 19 (2): for a goods application the value
-- of the goods or services and any damages for their detention are counted (fork F5: read as part of the amount claimed).
-- s 20: a person who would be entitled to claim more than $25 000 in a court of competent jurisdiction may abandon the
-- excess by limiting the claim to $25 000.
-- s 21: the parties agree, and the tribunal is satisfied the parties understand the excess is not being abandoned. Fork
-- F6: where both s 20 and s 21 are open, the s 21 answer (the whole amount) is given first.
@export Can ACAT hear my civil claim for this amount (ACAT Act 2008 ss 18-21)? ACAT's limit is $25 000, not counting interest. Above it, you can abandon the excess and claim $25 000 (s 20), or, if the parties agree and the tribunal is satisfied they understand the excess is not being given up, ACAT can decide the whole claim (s 21). The limit does not apply to a common boundaries determination or an application a regulation prescribes.
GIVEN `my claim` IS A `What my claim is` @desc What your claim is for
      `the total I claim` IS A NUMBER @desc The total amount you claim in dollars, including any interest (for a claim about goods or services, include the value of the goods or services and any damages for their detention; for a debt declaration, the debt)
      `of which interest` IS A NUMBER @desc How much of that total is interest, or a lump sum instead of interest (0 if none)
      `prescribed by regulation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a regulation under the Act prescribes this kind of application as outside the $25 000 limit (no regulation is deposited; answer no unless you know of one)
      `entitled in a court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you would be entitled to make the claim for the full amount in a court of competent jurisdiction
      `I abandon the excess` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you choose to limit your claim to $25 000, giving up the rest
      `the parties agree` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the parties agree to ACAT deciding the claim for more than $25 000
      `the tribunal is satisfied they understand` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal is satisfied that the parties understand the amount over $25 000 is not being abandoned
GIVETH A `Can ACAT hear my claim for this amount`
`ss 18-21 -- can ACAT hear my claim for this amount` `my claim` `the total I claim` `of which interest` `prescribed by regulation` `entitled in a court` `I abandon the excess` `the parties agree` `the tribunal is satisfied they understand` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`s 16 -- is my claim a civil dispute application` `my claim`))
        THEN `not a civil dispute application, so Part 4 and its limit do not apply (the text's no)`
        IF `my claim` EQUALS `a claim for a determination under the Common Boundaries Act 1981 (or a variation of one)` OR `prescribed by regulation`
        THEN `yes: the $25 000 limit does not apply to this application (s 18 (3))`
        IF `the amount counted` AT MOST `the jurisdictional limit (s 18 (2))`
        THEN `yes: the amount counted is not more than $25 000 (s 18 (2))`
        IF `the parties agree` AND `the tribunal is satisfied they understand`
        THEN `yes, the whole amount: the parties agree to ACAT deciding it and the tribunal is satisfied they understand the excess over $25 000 is not abandoned (s 21)`
        IF `entitled in a court` AND `I abandon the excess`
        THEN `yes, but only for $25 000: you abandon the excess by limiting your claim to $25 000, and the excess cannot be recovered (s 20)`
        OTHERWISE `no: the amount counted is more than $25 000 and neither s 20 nor s 21 allows it (the text's no, s 18 (1))`
    WHERE
        `the amount counted` MEANS `the total I claim` MINUS `of which interest`

-- s 82A: if, in dealing with a civil dispute application, the tribunal becomes aware that a matter arising (for example
-- a counter-claim) amounts to a fair work claim, it "must order that the application and the matter ... be removed to
-- the Magistrates Court"; the civil dispute application is then taken to be finalised in the tribunal. The "if" failing
-- is the text's no.
@export Must my civil dispute be moved from ACAT to the Magistrates Court because part of it is a fair work claim (ACAT Act 2008 s 82A)? If, while dealing with a civil dispute application, the tribunal becomes aware that a matter arising in it (such as a counter-claim) amounts to a fair work claim, it must remove the application and that matter to the Magistrates Court.
GIVEN `a civil dispute application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your application to ACAT is a civil dispute application (a debt, contract, goods, damages, nuisance or trespass claim and the like)
      `a matter arising is a fair work claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a matter arising in the application (for example a counter-claim) amounts to a fair work claim (a claim under the Fair Work Act 2009 that the Magistrates Court can hear)
      `the tribunal is aware of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal has become aware of that
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 82A -- must my application be removed to the Magistrates Court` `a civil dispute application` `a matter arising is a fair work claim` `the tribunal is aware of it` IF
        `a civil dispute application`
    AND `a matter arising is a fair work claim`
    AND `the tribunal is aware of it`

§§ `Part 4A -- Reasons statements and assistance in administrative review (ss 22B, 22D, 22T)`

-- s 22B (1): the section applies if a decision-maker makes a reviewable decision and, within 28 days after the day the
-- decision is made, a person who may apply for review asks the decision-maker in writing for a reasons statement. The
-- rules may prescribe a longer period for asking (s 25 (1) (e), (2)).
@export By when must I ask the decision-maker in writing for a statement of reasons for a decision ACAT can review (ACAT Act 2008 s 22B (1), s 25 (2))? Within 28 days after the day the decision is made, or a longer period the tribunal's rules prescribe.
GIVEN `the decision was made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision was made
      `days the rules allow` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the tribunal's rules allow for asking for a statement of reasons, if they prescribe a period longer than 28 days; otherwise 0
GIVETH A DATE
`s 22B -- the last day to ask for a reasons statement` `the decision was made on` `days the rules allow` MEANS
    `the decision was made on` `plus days` (`the days to apply for review` `days the rules allow` FALSE)

-- s 22B (2): "The decision-maker must give the applicant a written reasons statement for the decision within 28 days
-- after the day the applicant asks for the statement unless -- (a) the decision contains the matters that a reasons
-- statement would contain; or (b) a document that contains [them] has already been given to the applicant; or (c)
-- section 22E ... applies". Fork F7 (an "unless" once met, decided case by case): the duty does not arise -- the text's
-- no. For (c), s 22E (3) gives its own 28-day duty to tell the applicant (not encoded as a rule; named in the answer).
DECLARE `Must I be given a reasons statement` IS ONE OF
    `s 22B does not apply: the decision is not one you may apply to ACAT to review, or you did not ask in writing within the time allowed (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    `no reasons statement is required: the decision itself contains what a reasons statement would (s 22B (2) (a), the text's no)`
    `no reasons statement is required: a document containing what a reasons statement would has already been given to you (s 22B (2) (b), the text's no)`
    `not required in full: the Minister has certified that matter is not to be disclosed (s 22E); the decision-maker must tell you in writing within 28 days after the certificate what is left out and why (s 22E (3))`
    `given in time: within 28 days after the day you asked (s 22B (2))`
    `not yet given, and the 28 days after the day you asked have not ended (s 22B (2))`
    `late: the 28 days after the day you asked have ended without the statement (s 22B (2) not complied with)`

@export Must the decision-maker give me a written statement of reasons, and have they done so in time (ACAT Act 2008 s 22B)? If you may apply to ACAT for review of the decision and asked in writing within 28 days after it was made (or the longer period the rules allow), the decision-maker must give you a written reasons statement within 28 days after you asked, unless the decision or a document already given to you contains the reasons, or the Minister has certified under s 22E.
GIVEN `I may apply for review` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision is one you may apply to ACAT to have reviewed (a reviewable decision, and you are a person who may apply)
      `the decision was made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision was made
      `days the rules allow` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the tribunal's rules allow for asking for a statement of reasons, if longer than 28; otherwise 0
      `asked in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the decision-maker in writing for a statement of reasons
      `asked on` IS A DATE @desc The day you asked (if you did not, any date; it is not used)
      `the decision contains the reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision itself already contains what a statement of reasons would contain (findings on material questions of fact, the evidence relied on, and the reasons)
      `a document with the reasons was given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already been given a document that contains what a statement of reasons would contain
      `a Minister's certificate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Minister has certified under s 22E that disclosing a matter proposed for the statement is not in the public interest
      `given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision-maker has given you the statement
      `given on` IS A DATE @desc The day the statement was given (if it was not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Must I be given a reasons statement`
`s 22B -- must I be given a reasons statement` `I may apply for review` `the decision was made on` `days the rules allow` `asked in writing` `asked on` `the decision contains the reasons` `a document with the reasons was given` `a Minister's certificate` `given` `given on` `today` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`I may apply for review` AND `asked in writing` AND `asked in time`))
        THEN `s 22B does not apply: the decision is not one you may apply to ACAT to review, or you did not ask in writing within the time allowed (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
        IF `the decision contains the reasons`
        THEN `no reasons statement is required: the decision itself contains what a reasons statement would (s 22B (2) (a), the text's no)`
        IF `a document with the reasons was given`
        THEN `no reasons statement is required: a document containing what a reasons statement would has already been given to you (s 22B (2) (b), the text's no)`
        IF `a Minister's certificate`
        THEN `not required in full: the Minister has certified that matter is not to be disclosed (s 22E); the decision-maker must tell you in writing within 28 days after the certificate what is left out and why (s 22E (3))`
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `the timing under` `s 22B -- give the reasons statement within 28 days` `the decision-maker` `give the reasons statement` `given` `given on` `today` `asked on` (`asked on` `plus days` 28)
            WHEN `done in time` THEN `given in time: within 28 days after the day you asked (s 22B (2))`
            WHEN `not yet done, still in time` THEN `not yet given, and the 28 days after the day you asked have not ended (s 22B (2))`
            WHEN `out of time` THEN `late: the 28 days after the day you asked have ended without the statement (s 22B (2) not complied with)`
    WHERE
        `asked in time` MEANS
                (NOT (`asked on` `falls before` `the decision was made on`))
            AND (NOT ((`s 22B -- the last day to ask for a reasons statement` `the decision was made on` `days the rules allow`) `falls before` `asked on`))

-- s 22D (2): if the tribunal declares a reasons statement insufficient (s 22C), "The decision-maker must, within 28 days
-- after the day the tribunal makes the declaration, give the person who applied for the declaration an additional
-- statement". s 22D (3) (b): the reasons statement is taken to have been given when the additional statement is given.
@export ACAT has declared my reasons statement insufficient: by when must the decision-maker give me the additional statement (ACAT Act 2008 s 22D)? Within 28 days after the day the tribunal makes the declaration; the reasons statement is then taken to have been given only when the additional statement is given.
GIVEN `the declaration was made on` IS A DATE @desc The day ACAT declared under s 22C that the reasons statement you were given is not sufficient
GIVETH A DATE
`s 22D -- the last day for the additional statement` `the declaration was made on` MEANS
    `the declaration was made on` `plus days` 28

-- s 22T: a person who makes or proposes to make an application to the tribunal, or is a party to one, or to a court
-- proceeding for review, "in relation to a matter arising under this part" (Part 4A, administrative review) may apply
-- to the Minister for assistance; the Minister "may authorise ... legal or financial assistance ... if satisfied that
-- (a) it would involve hardship to the person to refuse the application; and (b) in all the circumstances, it is
-- reasonable that the application be granted", and may impose conditions. The Minister's satisfaction is asked.
-- "This section applies to a person who ..." is the section's own condition: when it fails, the text's no.
DECLARE `Can I get legal or financial assistance` IS ONE OF
    `the Minister may authorise legal or financial assistance by the Territory, on conditions the Minister sets; it is a discretion, not an entitlement (s 22T (3), (4))`
    `no assistance on these facts: the Minister is not satisfied both that refusing would be a hardship to you and that granting it is reasonable (the text's no, s 22T (3))`
    `you may apply to the Minister for assistance (s 22T (2)); you have not yet applied`
    `s 22T does not apply: the matter is not one arising under Part 4A (review of a decision) (the text's no)`

@export Can I get legal or financial help from the ACT Government for an ACAT review of a decision (ACAT Act 2008 s 22T)? If you make, propose or are a party to an application or court proceeding about a matter arising under Part 4A (administrative review), you may apply to the Minister, who may authorise legal or financial assistance if satisfied that refusing would be a hardship to you and that granting it is reasonable in all the circumstances.
GIVEN `a Part 4A matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you make or propose to make an application to ACAT (or are a party to one, or to a court proceeding for review) about a matter arising under Part 4A, the review of a government decision
      `applied to the Minister` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied to the Minister for assistance
      `hardship` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Minister is satisfied that refusing your application would involve hardship to you
      `reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Minister is satisfied that, in all the circumstances, it is reasonable that your application be granted
GIVETH A `Can I get legal or financial assistance`
`s 22T -- can I get legal or financial assistance` `a Part 4A matter` `applied to the Minister` `hardship` `reasonable` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`a Part 4A matter`))
        THEN `s 22T does not apply: the matter is not one arising under Part 4A (review of a decision) (the text's no)`
        IF (NOT (`applied to the Minister`))
        THEN `you may apply to the Minister for assistance (s 22T (2)); you have not yet applied`
        IF `hardship` AND `reasonable`
        THEN `the Minister may authorise legal or financial assistance by the Territory, on conditions the Minister sets; it is a discretion, not an entitlement (s 22T (3), (4))`
        OTHERWISE `no assistance on these facts: the Minister is not satisfied both that refusing would be a hardship to you and that granting it is reasonable (the text's no, s 22T (3))`

§§ `Part 5 -- Procedure: absence, costs, interim orders, deciding on the papers (ss 47A-54)`

-- Who the person is in the application.
DECLARE `My role` IS ONE OF
    `the applicant`
    `the respondent`
    `another party`

-- What a costs order can be for. s 48 (2) (a)-(d), s 48A.
DECLARE `Costs order` IS ONE OF
    `the filing fee, and any other fee the tribunal considers was necessary for the application (s 48 (2) (a))`
    `the reasonable costs arising from the unreasonable delay or obstruction (s 48 (2) (b))`
    `all or part of the costs of the application, on the Supreme Court scale, for contravening a tribunal order (s 48 (2) (c), s 49)`
    `the reasonable costs arising from a planning, heritage or urban forest review that was dismissed or struck out, including legal costs but not holding costs (s 48 (2) (d), (3))`
    `the reasonable costs arising from a freedom of information review in which access was ordered (s 48A)`

DECLARE `Kind of application` IS ONE OF
    `a civil dispute or another application`
    `a review of a decision under the Heritage Act 2004, the Planning Act 2023 or the Urban Forest Act 2023`
    `a review under the Freedom of Information Act 2016 s 84 of a refusal to give access`

GIVEN `g` IS A `Costs order`, `ok` IS A BOOLEAN, `rest` IS A LIST OF `Costs order`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Costs order`
`adding` `g` `if` `ok` `to` `rest` MEANS IF `ok` THEN `g` FOLLOWED BY `rest` ELSE `rest`

-- s 48 (1): "The parties to an application must bear their own costs unless this Act or another territory law otherwise
-- provides or the tribunal otherwise orders." s 48 (2): "However -- (a) if the tribunal decides an application in favour
-- of the applicant, the tribunal may order the other party to pay the applicant (i) the filing fee ... and (ii) any other
-- fee ... necessary ...; or (b) if the tribunal considers that a party ... caused unreasonable delay or obstruction ... the
-- tribunal may order the party to pay the reasonable costs of the other party arising from the delay or obstruction; or
-- (c) subject to section 49, if a party ... contravenes an order of the tribunal -- the tribunal may order the party to pay
-- the costs or part of the costs of the application to the other party; or (d) [planning, heritage, urban forest review
-- dismissed under s 32] the tribunal may order the applicant to pay the reasonable costs of the other party".
-- s 49 (1): costs under s 48 (2) (c) "only if satisfied that it is in the interests of justice"; (4) Supreme Court scale.
-- s 48A: FOI review, access ordered: the agency or Minister may be ordered to pay the applicant's reasonable costs.
-- Every order is a "may": the list is what the tribunal may order, never what it must. An empty list: none of these
-- particular orders is open, and each party bears its own costs "unless ... the tribunal otherwise orders" (s 48 (1));
-- that open discretion is not encoded beyond saying so (finding 6).
GIVEN `the receiver is the applicant` IS A BOOLEAN, `decided for the applicant` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the payer caused delay or obstruction` IS A BOOLEAN, `the payer contravened an order` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN, `kind` IS A `Kind of application`, `dismissed under s 32` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `access ordered` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A LIST OF `Costs order`
`the costs orders the tribunal may make for the receiver` `the receiver is the applicant` `decided for the applicant` `the payer caused delay or obstruction` `the payer contravened an order` `in the interests of justice` `kind` `dismissed under s 32` `access ordered` MEANS
    `adding` `the filing fee, and any other fee the tribunal considers was necessary for the application (s 48 (2) (a))` `if` (`the receiver is the applicant` AND `decided for the applicant`) `to` `the rest after 1`
    WHERE
        `the rest after 1` MEANS `adding` `the reasonable costs arising from the unreasonable delay or obstruction (s 48 (2) (b))` `if` `the payer caused delay or obstruction` `to` `the rest after 2`
        `the rest after 2` MEANS `adding` `all or part of the costs of the application, on the Supreme Court scale, for contravening a tribunal order (s 48 (2) (c), s 49)` `if` (`the payer contravened an order` AND `in the interests of justice`) `to` `the rest after 3`
        `the rest after 3` MEANS `adding` `the reasonable costs arising from a planning, heritage or urban forest review that was dismissed or struck out, including legal costs but not holding costs (s 48 (2) (d), (3))` `if` (`heritage review` AND `dismissed under s 32` AND (NOT (`the receiver is the applicant`))) `to` `the rest after 4`
        `the rest after 4` MEANS `adding` `the reasonable costs arising from a freedom of information review in which access was ordered (s 48A)` `if` (`FOI review` AND `access ordered` AND `the receiver is the applicant`) `to` EMPTY
        `heritage review` MEANS `kind` EQUALS `a review of a decision under the Heritage Act 2004, the Planning Act 2023 or the Urban Forest Act 2023`
        `FOI review` MEANS `kind` EQUALS `a review under the Freedom of Information Act 2016 s 84 of a refusal to give access`

@export What costs can ACAT order the other party to pay me (ACAT Act 2008 ss 48, 48A, 49)? Each party normally bears its own costs. The tribunal may order the other party to pay your filing and other necessary fees if it decides for you as applicant, costs caused by their unreasonable delay or obstruction, costs for their breach of a tribunal order (if in the interests of justice), costs of a dismissed planning, heritage or urban forest review you answered, or, in a freedom of information review where access is ordered, your reasonable costs. An empty list means none of these particular orders is open: each party bears its own costs unless the tribunal otherwise orders (s 48 (1)).
GIVEN `my role` IS A `My role` @desc Whether you are the applicant, the respondent or another party
      `kind` IS A `Kind of application` @desc What kind of application it is
      `decided in my favour` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal has decided the application in your favour
      `they caused delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal considers the other party caused unreasonable delay or obstruction before or while the tribunal dealt with the application
      `they breached an order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other party contravened (did not comply with) an order of the tribunal
      `in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal is satisfied that awarding costs against them for that contravention is in the interests of justice (considering whether it was deliberate or easily avoidable, how it affected a prompt hearing, and the importance of people being able to afford to bring applications)
      `dismissed under s 32` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal refused to hear, dismissed or struck out the application under s 32 (frivolous, vexatious, lacking in substance, an abuse of process)
      `access ordered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a freedom of information review: whether the tribunal ordered that you be given access to some or all of the information
GIVETH A LIST OF `Costs order`
`ss 48, 48A, 49 -- what costs can the other party be ordered to pay me` `my role` `kind` `decided in my favour` `they caused delay` `they breached an order` `in the interests of justice` `dismissed under s 32` `access ordered` MEANS
    `the costs orders the tribunal may make for the receiver` (`my role` EQUALS `the applicant`) `decided in my favour` `they caused delay` `they breached an order` `in the interests of justice` `kind` `dismissed under s 32` `access ordered`

@export What costs can ACAT order me to pay the other party (ACAT Act 2008 ss 48, 49)? Each party normally bears its own costs. You may be ordered to pay the applicant's filing and necessary fees if the tribunal decides against you as respondent, costs caused by your unreasonable delay or obstruction, costs for your breach of a tribunal order (if in the interests of justice), or, if your planning, heritage or urban forest review is dismissed or struck out under s 32, the other party's reasonable costs. An empty list means none of these particular orders is open: each party bears its own costs unless the tribunal otherwise orders (s 48 (1)).
GIVEN `my role` IS A `My role` @desc Whether you are the applicant, the respondent or another party
      `kind` IS A `Kind of application` @desc What kind of application it is
      `decided against me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal has decided the application in favour of the applicant, against you
      `I caused delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal considers you caused unreasonable delay or obstruction before or while the tribunal dealt with the application
      `I breached an order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you contravened (did not comply with) an order of the tribunal
      `in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal is satisfied that awarding costs against you for that contravention is in the interests of justice
      `dismissed under s 32` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal refused to hear, dismissed or struck out your application under s 32
GIVETH A LIST OF `Costs order`
`ss 48, 49 -- what costs can I be ordered to pay` `my role` `kind` `decided against me` `I caused delay` `I breached an order` `in the interests of justice` `dismissed under s 32` MEANS
    `the costs orders the tribunal may make for the receiver` (NOT (`my role` EQUALS `the applicant`)) (`decided against me` AND (NOT (`my role` EQUALS `the applicant`))) `I caused delay` `I breached an order` `in the interests of justice` `kind` (`dismissed under s 32` AND `my role` EQUALS `the applicant`) FALSE

-- What can happen to a party who does not appear (s 47A) or does not comply with an order (s 74, s 48 (2) (c)).
DECLARE `What can happen to me` IS ONE OF
    `your application may be dismissed (s 47A (2) (a))`
    `the application may be decided without you (s 47A (2) (b))`
    `you may be removed as a party (s 47A (2) (c))`
    `the hearing may go on without you, be set down for another time, or other orders or steps may be ordered (s 47A (2) (d))`
    `you may apply to have an order made in your absence amended or set aside (s 56 (c) (i))`
    `you may be ordered to pay the Territory a stated amount, up to any amount a regulation prescribes (s 74 (2) (a))`
    `your application may be struck out (s 74 (2) (b))`
    `an order may be made in favour of the applicant (s 74 (2) (c))`
    `you may be ordered to pay all or part of the other party's costs of the application, if the tribunal is satisfied it is in the interests of justice (s 48 (2) (c), s 49)`

GIVEN `g` IS A `What can happen to me`, `ok` IS A BOOLEAN, `rest` IS A LIST OF `What can happen to me`
GIVETH A LIST OF `What can happen to me`
`adding consequence` `g` `if` `ok` `to` `rest` MEANS IF `ok` THEN `g` FOLLOWED BY `rest` ELSE `rest`

-- s 47A (1): applies if the tribunal requires a party to appear and the party fails to appear; (2) the tribunal may
-- dismiss (applicant), decide (respondent), remove (another party), or continue, adjourn, order steps or make other
-- orders. s 56 (c) (i): an order made after hearing in a party's absence may be amended or set aside.
-- s 74 (1): "A party to an application must not, without reasonable excuse, fail to comply with a tribunal order." (2):
-- the tribunal may order a payment to the Territory (capped by any amount a regulation prescribes; none deposited),
-- strike out the applicant's application, or make an order in favour of the applicant against the respondent.
-- s 48 (2) (c) with s 49: costs for contravening an order. Fork F8: s 48 (2) (c) says "contravenes", with no reasonable
-- excuse; a reasonable excuse is left to the tribunal's interests-of-justice judgment (s 49 (2) (a)), asked as a fact.
@export What can ACAT do to me if I do not turn up when required, or do not comply with a tribunal order (ACAT Act 2008 ss 47A, 74, 48 (2) (c), 49, 56 (c))? Each item is something the tribunal may do, not must do. An empty list means these sections give the tribunal no such power on your facts.
GIVEN `my role` IS A `My role` @desc Whether you are the applicant, the respondent or another party
      `failed to appear` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal required you to appear (in person or by a representative) and you did not
      `failed to comply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you failed to comply with an order of the tribunal
      `reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had a reasonable excuse for not complying with the order
      `in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal is satisfied that awarding costs against you for not complying is in the interests of justice
GIVETH A LIST OF `What can happen to me`
`ss 47A, 74 -- what can happen if I do not appear or do not comply` `my role` `failed to appear` `failed to comply` `reasonable excuse` `in the interests of justice` MEANS
    `adding consequence` `your application may be dismissed (s 47A (2) (a))` `if` (`failed to appear` AND `applicant`) `to` `the rest after 1`
    WHERE
        `the rest after 1` MEANS `adding consequence` `the application may be decided without you (s 47A (2) (b))` `if` (`failed to appear` AND `respondent`) `to` `the rest after 2`
        `the rest after 2` MEANS `adding consequence` `you may be removed as a party (s 47A (2) (c))` `if` (`failed to appear` AND `my role` EQUALS `another party`) `to` `the rest after 3`
        `the rest after 3` MEANS `adding consequence` `the hearing may go on without you, be set down for another time, or other orders or steps may be ordered (s 47A (2) (d))` `if` `failed to appear` `to` `the rest after 4`
        `the rest after 4` MEANS `adding consequence` `you may apply to have an order made in your absence amended or set aside (s 56 (c) (i))` `if` `failed to appear` `to` `the rest after 5`
        `the rest after 5` MEANS `adding consequence` `you may be ordered to pay the Territory a stated amount, up to any amount a regulation prescribes (s 74 (2) (a))` `if` `s 74 contravened` `to` `the rest after 6`
        `the rest after 6` MEANS `adding consequence` `your application may be struck out (s 74 (2) (b))` `if` (`s 74 contravened` AND `applicant`) `to` `the rest after 7`
        `the rest after 7` MEANS `adding consequence` `an order may be made in favour of the applicant (s 74 (2) (c))` `if` (`s 74 contravened` AND `respondent`) `to` `the rest after 8`
        `the rest after 8` MEANS `adding consequence` `you may be ordered to pay all or part of the other party's costs of the application, if the tribunal is satisfied it is in the interests of justice (s 48 (2) (c), s 49)` `if` (`failed to comply` AND `in the interests of justice`) `to` EMPTY
        `applicant` MEANS `my role` EQUALS `the applicant`
        `respondent` MEANS `my role` EQUALS `the respondent`
        `s 74 contravened` MEANS `failed to comply` AND (NOT (`reasonable excuse`))

-- s 53 (1): applies if, before the application is finalised, a party applies for an interim order and "the tribunal is
-- satisfied that, if an order ... were not made, the party applying for the order would be disadvantaged or suffer
-- harm"; (2) the tribunal "may make any order ... it considers appropriate to protect the position of the party"; (3) it
-- lasts until the tribunal orders otherwise or the application is finalised.
DECLARE `Can I get an interim order` IS ONE OF
    `the tribunal may make any interim order it considers appropriate to protect your position; it lasts until the tribunal orders otherwise or the application is finalised (s 53 (2), (3))`
    `s 53 does not apply: the application has been finalised, you have not applied, or the tribunal is not satisfied you would be disadvantaged or harmed without it (the text's no: its "if" fails)`

@export Can ACAT make an interim order to protect my position before my application is decided (ACAT Act 2008 s 53)? If, before the application is finalised, you apply for one and the tribunal is satisfied that without it you would be disadvantaged or suffer harm, the tribunal may make any interim order it considers appropriate.
GIVEN `not yet finalised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your application is still before the tribunal (not yet finalised)
      `I applied for one` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied to the tribunal for an interim order
      `disadvantage or harm` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal is satisfied that, without the order, you would be disadvantaged or suffer harm
GIVETH A `Can I get an interim order`
`s 53 -- can I get an interim order` `not yet finalised` `I applied for one` `disadvantage or harm` MEANS
    IF `not yet finalised` AND `I applied for one` AND `disadvantage or harm`
    THEN `the tribunal may make any interim order it considers appropriate to protect your position; it lasts until the tribunal orders otherwise or the application is finalised (s 53 (2), (3))`
    ELSE `s 53 does not apply: the application has been finalised, you have not applied, or the tribunal is not satisfied you would be disadvantaged or harmed without it (the text's no: its "if" fails)`

-- s 54 (2) (b): the party must make representations about a proposal to decide on the documents "within -- (i) 21 days
-- after the day the notice is given; or (ii) if the tribunal decides that a shorter period is required ... the shorter
-- period". s 80 (1) (c): representations about the president's proposal to dismiss an appeal "within 21 days after the
-- day the notice is given". The rules may prescribe a longer period for either (s 25 (1) (e), (2)). Fork F9: a shorter
-- period under s 54 is counted from the same day; s 80 has no shorter period.
DECLARE `Which notice` IS ONE OF
    `a notice that the tribunal proposes to decide my application on the documents, without a hearing (s 54 (2))`
    `a notice that the president proposes to dismiss my appeal as substantively similar to appeals already rejected (s 80 (1))`

@export By when must I make my representations after a notice that ACAT proposes to decide my application without a hearing, or that the president proposes to dismiss my appeal (ACAT Act 2008 s 54 (2), s 80 (1), s 25 (2))? Within 21 days after the day the notice is given, or a shorter period the tribunal decides (s 54 only), or a longer period the rules prescribe.
GIVEN `which notice` IS A `Which notice` @desc Which notice you were given
      `notice given on` IS A DATE @desc The day the notice was given to you
      `shorter period` IS A NUMBER @desc For a notice under s 54: the number of days, if the tribunal decided a period shorter than 21 days is required; otherwise 0
      `days the rules allow` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the tribunal's rules allow for these representations, if they prescribe a period longer than 21 days; otherwise 0
GIVETH A DATE
`ss 54, 80 -- the last day for my representations` `which notice` `notice given on` `shorter period` `days the rules allow` MEANS
    `notice given on` `plus days` `the days`
    WHERE
        `the days` MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF `days the rules allow` GREATER THAN 21
                THEN `days the rules allow`
                IF `which notice` EQUALS `a notice that the tribunal proposes to decide my application on the documents, without a hearing (s 54 (2))` AND `shorter period` GREATER THAN 0 AND `shorter period` LESS THAN 21
                THEN `shorter period`
                OTHERWISE 21

§§ `Part 6 -- The tribunal's order: copy, reasons, review outcomes (ss 59, 60)`

DECLARE `Kind of order` IS ONE OF
    `an order deciding the application, or another order that is not procedural`
    `an interim order under s 53`
    `an order of a procedural nature (an adjournment, default judgment, or joining a party)`

-- s 59 (1) (b): the tribunal must "give each party a copy of the order within 7 days after the day the tribunal makes the
-- order"; (2), (3): a party may ask within 7 days and must be given a copy within 7 days after asking; (4) not for an
-- interim order under s 53 (a flat "does not apply": the text's no).
DECLARE `When must I get a copy of the order` IS ONE OF
    `by this day` HAS `the last day` IS A DATE
    `s 59 does not apply to an interim order under s 53 (the text's no)`

@export By when must ACAT give me a copy of its order (ACAT Act 2008 s 59)? Within 7 days after the day the order is made; if you asked for a copy of the details within 7 days after it was made, within 7 days after you asked if that is later. Not for interim orders.
GIVEN `kind of order` IS A `Kind of order` @desc What kind of order the tribunal made
      `made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the tribunal made the order
      `I asked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the tribunal for a copy of the details of the order
      `asked on` IS A DATE @desc The day you asked (if you did not, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A `When must I get a copy of the order`
`s 59 -- by when must I get a copy of the order` `kind of order` `made on` `I asked` `asked on` MEANS
    IF `kind of order` EQUALS `an interim order under s 53`
    THEN `s 59 does not apply to an interim order under s 53 (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `I asked` AND (NOT (`asked on` `falls before` `made on`)) AND (NOT ((`made on` `plus days` 7) `falls before` `asked on`))
         THEN `by this day` (`the later of` (`made on` `plus days` 7) `and` (`asked on` `plus days` 7))
         ELSE `by this day` (`made on` `plus days` 7)

-- s 60 (1), (2): if, within 14 days after the day the order is made, a party asks for a statement of reasons, "The
-- tribunal must give the party a written statement of reasons or a transcript of an oral statement of reasons"; (4)
-- not for an interim order or an order of a procedural nature. The rules may prescribe a longer period (note to s 60;
-- s 25 (1) (e), (2)). No time is set for the tribunal to give the statement (finding 5).
DECLARE `Can I get the tribunal's reasons` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the tribunal must give you a written statement of reasons, or a transcript of its oral reasons (s 60 (2))`
    `not yet asked: you may ask, by this day` HAS `the last day` IS A DATE
    `no: you asked after the time allowed (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    `no: s 60 does not apply to an interim order or an order of a procedural nature (the text's no, s 60 (4))`

@export Can I get a statement of reasons for ACAT's order, and by when must I ask (ACAT Act 2008 s 60)? If you ask within 14 days after the day the order is made (or the longer period the rules allow), the tribunal must give you a written statement of reasons or a transcript of its oral reasons; not for interim or procedural orders.
GIVEN `kind of order` IS A `Kind of order` @desc What kind of order the tribunal made
      `made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the tribunal made the order
      `days the rules allow` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the tribunal's rules allow for asking, if they prescribe a period longer than 14 days; otherwise 0
      `I asked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have asked the tribunal for a statement of reasons
      `asked on` IS A DATE @desc The day you asked (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A `Can I get the tribunal's reasons`
`s 60 -- can I get the tribunal's reasons` `kind of order` `made on` `days the rules allow` `I asked` `asked on` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`kind of order` EQUALS `an order deciding the application, or another order that is not procedural`))
        THEN `no: s 60 does not apply to an interim order or an order of a procedural nature (the text's no, s 60 (4))`
        IF (NOT (`I asked`))
        THEN `not yet asked: you may ask, by this day` `the last day to ask`
        IF `the last day to ask` `falls before` `asked on`
        THEN `no: you asked after the time allowed (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the tribunal must give you a written statement of reasons, or a transcript of its oral reasons (s 60 (2))`
    WHERE
        `the days` MEANS IF `days the rules allow` GREATER THAN 14 THEN `days the rules allow` ELSE 14
        `the last day to ask` MEANS `made on` `plus days` `the days`

-- s 67A (1), (2) and s 67B (1), (2): a person who makes a reviewable decision (or an internally reviewable decision)
-- "must take reasonable steps to give written notice ... of the decision to any person whose interests are affected by
-- the decision", except a decision not to impose a liability, penalty or limitation on a person that does not adversely
-- affect another's interests. (3): a regulation may prescribe the time and contents (none deposited). (4): failure does
-- not affect the decision's validity. "Does not apply" is a flat negative: the text's no.
DECLARE `Kind of decision` IS ONE OF
    `a decision ACAT can review`
    `a decision that must or may be internally reviewed or reconsidered before ACAT review`
    `a decision ACAT cannot review`

DECLARE `Must I be told of the decision` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the decision-maker must take reasonable steps to give you written notice (a reviewable decision notice, s 67A); a regulation may set its time and contents; if it is not given, the decision is still valid`
    `yes: the decision-maker must take reasonable steps to give you written notice (an internal review notice, s 67B); a regulation may set its time and contents; if it is not given, the decision is still valid`
    `no: the decision does not impose a liability, penalty or limitation and does not adversely affect anyone else (the text's no, s 67A (2), s 67B (2))`
    `no: your interests are not affected by the decision (the text's no)`
    `ss 67A and 67B do not apply to a decision ACAT cannot review (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Must I be given written notice of a government decision that ACAT can review (ACAT Act 2008 ss 67A, 67B)? The person who makes a reviewable (or internally reviewable) decision must take reasonable steps to give written notice to anyone whose interests are affected, unless the decision imposes no liability, penalty or limitation and adversely affects no-one else.
GIVEN `kind of decision` IS A `Kind of decision` @desc Whether the decision is one ACAT can review, one that must or may first be internally reviewed or reconsidered, or neither
      `my interests are affected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision affects your interests
      `imposes nothing and harms no-one` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision is a decision not to impose a liability, penalty or limitation on someone, and does not adversely affect anyone else's interests
GIVETH A `Must I be told of the decision`
`ss 67A, 67B -- must I be told of the decision` `kind of decision` `my interests are affected` `imposes nothing and harms no-one` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `kind of decision` EQUALS `a decision ACAT cannot review`
        THEN `ss 67A and 67B do not apply to a decision ACAT cannot review (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF (NOT (`my interests are affected`))
        THEN `no: your interests are not affected by the decision (the text's no)`
        IF `imposes nothing and harms no-one`
        THEN `no: the decision does not impose a liability, penalty or limitation and does not adversely affect anyone else (the text's no, s 67A (2), s 67B (2))`
        IF `kind of decision` EQUALS `a decision ACAT can review`
        THEN `yes: the decision-maker must take reasonable steps to give you written notice (a reviewable decision notice, s 67A); a regulation may set its time and contents; if it is not given, the decision is still valid`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the decision-maker must take reasonable steps to give you written notice (an internal review notice, s 67B); a regulation may set its time and contents; if it is not given, the decision is still valid`

§§ `Part 7 -- Enforcing the order (ss 69A, 71)`

-- s 71 (1): a money order or non-money order "may be enforced by filing in the appropriate court -- (a) a copy of the
-- order sealed by the tribunal; and (b) a supporting affidavit stating -- (i) for a money order -- the amount owed ...; and
-- (ii) for a non-money order -- non-compliance". s 69A: the appropriate court is the Magistrates Court if the amount or
-- relief is within its jurisdiction, otherwise the Supreme Court. The Magistrates Court's limit (Magistrates Court Act
-- 1930, not deposited) is asked.
DECLARE `Kind of order to enforce` IS ONE OF
    `an order to pay money`
    `an order to do or not do something else`

DECLARE `Where and how I enforce the order` IS ONE OF
    `file in the Magistrates Court a sealed copy of the order and an affidavit stating the amount owed (ss 69A (a), 71)`
    `file in the Magistrates Court a sealed copy of the order and an affidavit stating the non-compliance (ss 69A (a), 71)`
    `file in the Supreme Court a sealed copy of the order and an affidavit stating the amount owed (ss 69A (b), 71)`
    `file in the Supreme Court a sealed copy of the order and an affidavit stating the non-compliance (ss 69A (b), 71)`

@export Where and how do I enforce an ACAT order the other party has not complied with (ACAT Act 2008 ss 69A, 71)? File a copy of the order sealed by the tribunal, with an affidavit stating the amount owed (money order) or the non-compliance (other order), in the Magistrates Court if the amount or relief is within its jurisdiction, otherwise in the Supreme Court; the order is then enforceable as that court's order.
GIVEN `kind of order` IS A `Kind of order to enforce` @desc Whether the order is to pay money or to do (or not do) something else
      `within the Magistrates Court's jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the amount payable, or the relief the order gives, is within the Magistrates Court's civil jurisdiction (under the Magistrates Court Act 1930)
GIVETH A `Where and how I enforce the order`
`ss 69A, 71 -- where and how do I enforce the order` `kind of order` `within the Magistrates Court's jurisdiction` MEANS
    IF `within the Magistrates Court's jurisdiction`
    THEN IF `kind of order` EQUALS `an order to pay money`
         THEN `file in the Magistrates Court a sealed copy of the order and an affidavit stating the amount owed (ss 69A (a), 71)`
         ELSE `file in the Magistrates Court a sealed copy of the order and an affidavit stating the non-compliance (ss 69A (a), 71)`
    ELSE IF `kind of order` EQUALS `an order to pay money`
         THEN `file in the Supreme Court a sealed copy of the order and an affidavit stating the amount owed (ss 69A (b), 71)`
         ELSE `file in the Supreme Court a sealed copy of the order and an affidavit stating the non-compliance (ss 69A (b), 71)`

§§ `Part 8 -- Appeals and removal (ss 79, 83, 86)`

-- The kind of application, for the appeal routes.
DECLARE `Kind of application appealed` IS ONE OF
    `any other application (a civil dispute, or a review under another law)`
    `a review of a decision under the Freedom of Information Act 2016`
    `a heritage, planning or urban forest review (Heritage Act 2004, Planning Act 2023, Urban Forest Act 2023)`
    `an external review of an insurer's decision under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2019, division 2.10.3`

-- s 79 (1): applies if the tribunal has decided an application and the application was not an appeal from a decision by
-- the tribunal; (2): not for an FOI, heritage, planning or urban forest review; (3): "A party to the original
-- application may, by application, appeal the decision to the tribunal on a question of fact or law." The Act sets no
-- time for the appeal: the rules do (not deposited; finding 4).
DECLARE `Can I appeal within ACAT` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may appeal to the appeal tribunal on a question of fact or law (s 79 (3)); the time for appealing is set by the tribunal's rules, not the Act`
    `no appeal within ACAT for a freedom of information, heritage, planning or urban forest review (the text's no, s 79 (2)); see s 86 for the Supreme Court`
    `no appeal within ACAT from a decision that was itself on appeal (the text's no: its "if" fails); see s 86 for the Supreme Court`
    `not yet: the tribunal has not decided the application (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    `s 79 gives the appeal to a party to the original application only (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can I appeal an ACAT decision to the ACAT appeal tribunal (ACAT Act 2008 s 79)? A party to an application the tribunal has decided may appeal on a question of fact or law, unless the decision was itself on appeal or the application was a freedom of information, heritage, planning or urban forest review.
GIVEN `I was a party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a party to the application the tribunal decided
      `decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal has decided the application
      `it was itself an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision you want to appeal was made on an appeal from an earlier tribunal decision
      `kind` IS A `Kind of application appealed` @desc What kind of application the tribunal decided
GIVETH A `Can I appeal within ACAT`
`s 79 -- can I appeal within ACAT` `I was a party` `decided` `it was itself an appeal` `kind` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`decided`))
        THEN `not yet: the tribunal has not decided the application (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
        IF `it was itself an appeal`
        THEN `no appeal within ACAT from a decision that was itself on appeal (the text's no: its "if" fails); see s 86 for the Supreme Court`
        IF `kind` EQUALS `a review of a decision under the Freedom of Information Act 2016` OR `kind` EQUALS `a heritage, planning or urban forest review (Heritage Act 2004, Planning Act 2023, Urban Forest Act 2023)`
        THEN `no appeal within ACAT for a freedom of information, heritage, planning or urban forest review (the text's no, s 79 (2)); see s 86 for the Supreme Court`
        IF (NOT (`I was a party`))
        THEN `s 79 gives the appeal to a party to the original application only (NOTHING-kind)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: you may appeal to the appeal tribunal on a question of fact or law (s 79 (3)); the time for appealing is set by the tribunal's rules, not the Act`

-- s 86 (1): a party to an application (other than one in (2) or (3)) may appeal to the Supreme Court on a question of fact
-- or law from (a) (i) a decision of the appeal tribunal, (ii) the original decision if the president dismissed the appeal
-- under s 80, (iii) the original decision if the president decided not to deal with the appeal under s 85; or (b) a
-- decision on an FOI review. (2): heritage, planning, urban forest review: on a question of law from the original
-- decision. (3): MAI external review: on a question of law from a decision of the appeal tribunal under MAI Act s 199.
-- (4): "However, the appeal may be brought only with the Supreme Court's leave." Fork F10: (4) read as applying to every
-- appeal under s 86. Fork F11: a decision not in the list for the kind of application -- NOTHING-kind (s 86 gives no
-- appeal from it; the list is not closed with "only").
DECLARE `What I appeal from` IS ONE OF
    `a decision of the appeal tribunal`
    `the original decision, after the president dismissed my appeal under s 80`
    `the original decision, after the president decided not to deal with my appeal under s 85`
    `the original decision, without an appeal within ACAT`

DECLARE `Question raised` IS ONE OF
    `a question of law`
    `a question of fact only`

DECLARE `Can I appeal to the Supreme Court` IS ONE OF
    `yes, on a question of fact or law, but only with the Supreme Court's leave (s 86 (1), (4))`
    `yes, on a question of law, but only with the Supreme Court's leave (s 86 (2) or (3), (4))`
    `no: for this kind of application s 86 allows an appeal on a question of law only (the text's no)`
    `s 86 gives no appeal to the Supreme Court from that decision; appeal within ACAT first (s 79) (NOTHING-kind)`
    `s 86 gives the appeal to a party to the application only (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can I appeal an ACAT decision to the ACT Supreme Court (ACAT Act 2008 s 86)? Usually only from the appeal tribunal's decision (or the original decision if the president dismissed or declined to deal with your internal appeal), on a question of fact or law; from a freedom of information review decision directly; for heritage, planning and urban forest reviews and motor accident injuries reviews, on a question of law only. Every such appeal needs the Supreme Court's leave.
GIVEN `I was a party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a party to the application
      `kind` IS A `Kind of application appealed` @desc What kind of application it was
      `from` IS A `What I appeal from` @desc Which decision you want to appeal from
      `question` IS A `Question raised` @desc Whether your appeal raises a question of law (with or without questions of fact), or a question of fact only
GIVETH A `Can I appeal to the Supreme Court`
`s 86 -- can I appeal to the Supreme Court` `I was a party` `kind` `from` `question` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`I was a party`))
        THEN `s 86 gives the appeal to a party to the application only (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF (NOT (`a decision s 86 lists`))
        THEN `s 86 gives no appeal to the Supreme Court from that decision; appeal within ACAT first (s 79) (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF `law only` AND `question` EQUALS `a question of fact only`
        THEN `no: for this kind of application s 86 allows an appeal on a question of law only (the text's no)`
        IF `law only`
        THEN `yes, on a question of law, but only with the Supreme Court's leave (s 86 (2) or (3), (4))`
        OTHERWISE `yes, on a question of fact or law, but only with the Supreme Court's leave (s 86 (1), (4))`
    WHERE
        `law only` MEANS
                `kind` EQUALS `a heritage, planning or urban forest review (Heritage Act 2004, Planning Act 2023, Urban Forest Act 2023)`
            OR  `kind` EQUALS `an external review of an insurer's decision under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2019, division 2.10.3`
        `a decision s 86 lists` MEANS
            CONSIDER `kind`
            WHEN `any other application (a civil dispute, or a review under another law)` THEN (NOT (`from` EQUALS `the original decision, without an appeal within ACAT`))
            WHEN `a review of a decision under the Freedom of Information Act 2016` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `a heritage, planning or urban forest review (Heritage Act 2004, Planning Act 2023, Urban Forest Act 2023)` THEN `from` EQUALS `the original decision, without an appeal within ACAT`
            WHEN `an external review of an insurer's decision under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2019, division 2.10.3` THEN `from` EQUALS `a decision of the appeal tribunal`

-- s 83 (1): if the parties jointly apply to have the matter removed to the Supreme Court, the tribunal "must order" it;
-- (2) if one party applies, the tribunal "may, if it considers it appropriate"; (3) not for an MAI application or an
-- appeal from one (a flat "does not apply": the text's no).
DECLARE `Can my matter be removed to the Supreme Court` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the parties applied jointly, so the tribunal must order removal (s 83 (1))`
    `yes: you applied and the tribunal considers removal appropriate (s 83 (2))`
    `no: you applied alone and the tribunal does not consider removal appropriate (the text's no, s 83 (2))`
    `no: s 83 does not apply to a motor accident injuries review or an appeal from one (the text's no, s 83 (3))`
    `s 83 does not arise: no party has applied for removal (the text's no: its "if" fails)`

@export Can my ACAT application or appeal be moved to the Supreme Court (ACAT Act 2008 s 83)? If all the parties apply jointly, the tribunal must order removal; if one party applies, it may if it considers it appropriate. Not for motor accident injuries reviews.
GIVEN `MAI review` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter is an external review of an insurer's decision under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2019, division 2.10.3, or an appeal from one
      `jointly` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the parties have jointly applied to have the matter removed
      `I applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (or another party alone) have applied to have the matter removed
      `appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal considers removal appropriate
GIVETH A `Can my matter be removed to the Supreme Court`
`s 83 -- can my matter be removed to the Supreme Court` `MAI review` `jointly` `I applied` `appropriate` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `MAI review`
        THEN `no: s 83 does not apply to a motor accident injuries review or an appeal from one (the text's no, s 83 (3))`
        IF `jointly`
        THEN `yes: the parties applied jointly, so the tribunal must order removal (s 83 (1))`
        IF (NOT (`I applied`))
        THEN `s 83 does not arise: no party has applied for removal (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
        IF `appropriate`
        THEN `yes: you applied and the tribunal considers removal appropriate (s 83 (2))`
        OTHERWISE `no: you applied alone and the tribunal does not consider removal appropriate (the text's no, s 83 (2))`
